Subject: Fuse A Local-Keyed Element Load Into One Opcode

What this does
  `obj[k]`, where k is a local, compiles to push the receiver, load the local and
  `get_array_el`. The peephole folds the last two into `OP_get_loc8_array_el n`,
  which takes the receiver from the stack and reads the key out of the local
  frame.

Why we need it
  The middle instruction is pure glue: an element load whose key is already a
  local was paying a second dispatch, a stack push and pop for the key, and a
  dup/free refcount round trip on it. Prop-driven code reads `x[key]` once per
  property per object, so those costs land on a hot path.

What it adds
  One opcode, in the slot of the retired `OP_put_arg1`. The key is borrowed
  rather than duplicated, so the handler reads it through a property path that
  never frees it. A plain dense array is served inline; every other receiver --
  typed arrays, arguments objects, proxies, holes and lazily materialised slots,
  and anything the fast path refuses -- takes the same generic property read the
  unfused pair used, so behaviour is unchanged. The handler is written to keep
  JS_CallInternal's frame small: maximum recursion depth is unchanged at 1039
  frames. `put_arg1` is no longer emitted, so a write to a named parameter other
  than the first takes the 3-byte general form, and both the built-in bytecode
  blobs and the committed host compilers are rebuilt for the new numbering.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  Yes. `9999` raises the version number and carries the rebuilt built-in blobs.


